00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.950
<v Orlando>This is <lang pt-BR>Tá Falado</lang>, and it is lesson number 16
on the placement of indirect object pronouns.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.950
<v Orlando>Putting them between two, two verbs.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:21.950
<v Valdo>Right.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.950
<v Orlando>My name is Orlando Kelm, and I'm here with the normal team.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:24.950
<v Valdo>Valdo Oliveira.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:25.950
<v Michelle>Michelle Lima.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.950
<v José Luis>And José Luis Montiel.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.950
<v Orlando>Which is the last but not least.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.100
<v José Luis>Thank you. <lang es>Gracias.</lang>

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:38.500
<v Orlando>In fact, today we're talking about greeting friends and
how Brazilians versus North Americans greet each other.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.140
<v Orlando>And so we'll get into that topic as
we get into the dialogue as well.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:50.190
<v Orlando>What you're going to hear is the Brazilian tendency
to put the indirect object pronoun between verbs.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:56.508
<v Orlando>And so in the very first line you're going to
hear, <lang pt-BR>vai nos apresentar</lang>, <lang pt-BR>vai nos apresentar</lang>,

00:00:56.508 --> 00:00:58.850
<v Orlando>which means going to introduce us.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:00:59.850
<v Valdo>Us.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:05.010
<v Orlando>Now in Spanish you couldn't say <lang es>va</lang>
<lang es>nos presentar</lang>. You would have to say…

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.040
<v José Luis><lang es>Quisiera presentarle.</lang>

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.850
<v José Luis><lang es>Presentarle. Nos va a presentar.</lang>

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.750
<v Orlando>That you have to put it before or after
the two verbs, but not in between.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:19.500
<v Orlando>You're going to hear the phrase, <lang pt-BR>querem</lang>
<lang pt-BR>te beijar</lang>. They want to kiss you.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.710
<v Orlando>But in Spanish, you couldn't say <lang es>quieren te besar</lang>.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:24.210
<v José Luis>No.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.750
<v Orlando>You'd have to say instead…

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.420
<v José Luis><lang es>Yo quisiera besarte. Besarte.</lang>

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.250
<v Orlando>Put it afterwards.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:29.780
<v Valdo>Right.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:38.800
<v Orlando>You're going to hear the phrase <lang pt-BR>vem me dar</lang>, come
to give me, <lang pt-BR>vem me dar</lang>. And you'd have to say…

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.050
<v José Luis><lang es>Ven a darme.</lang>

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.660
<v Orlando><lang es>Ven a darme</lang>, put it afterwards.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:48.080
<v Orlando>And so for a Spanish brain, it takes a little while to get
used to hearing the use of <lang pt-BR>nos</lang>, <lang pt-BR>me</lang>, and <lang pt-BR>te</lang> between verbs.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.750
<v Valdo>Between verbs, yeah. But it sounds really natural in Portuguese.

